Backend Components (Detailed)
Below is a file-level breakdown of the backend architecture for Shramik, a platform designed to connect skilled workers with customers.

Routes (/backend/src/routes)
auth.ts — handles registration and login endpoints (/api/auth/*) for workers and customers.
workers.ts — manages worker profiles, skills, trade categories, availability, and worker discovery (/api/workers/*).
customers.ts — handles customer profiles and customer-related operations (/api/customers/*).
bookings.ts — manages booking creation, confirmation, status updates, and booking history (/api/bookings/*).
jobs.ts — handles job listings, job discovery, and worker job applications (/api/jobs/*).
reviews.ts — manages customer reviews and ratings for completed services (/api/reviews/*).
locations.ts — handles area selection and location-related search (/api/locations/*).
chat.ts — provides the backend endpoint for Shramik Sahayak AI conversations (/api/chat).
These are proposed route names; include only endpoints implemented in your actual backend.

Services (/backend/src/services)
authService.ts — handles authentication business logic, password verification, and session or token creation.
workerService.ts — manages worker profiles, skills, trade categories, and availability.
customerService.ts — manages customer profile information and customer-related operations.
bookingService.ts — processes booking requests, availability checks, and booking status changes.
jobService.ts — manages job creation, job discovery, and job-related operations.
chatService.ts — connects the backend to the Gemini API and processes Shramik Sahayak requests.
Background Jobs & Notifications
Background processing can be added as Shramik grows to support:

Booking reminders and status notifications.
Availability updates.
Scheduled cleanup of outdated data.
Other asynchronous tasks that should not delay API responses.
A queue system such as BullMQ with Redis may be introduced if background processing becomes necessary.

AI Assistant Integration
chatService.ts — prepares user messages and communicates with Gemini.
chat.ts — exposes the chat endpoint to the frontend.
Environment configuration — reads GEMINI_API_KEY on the server.
Error handling — manages API failures and unavailable AI responses.
The API key should never be exposed in frontend code or committed to the repository.

Location & Worker Discovery
locations.ts — accepts location or area search requests.
locationUtils.ts — can contain location normalization and distance-calculation helpers.
workerService.ts — retrieves workers matching a requested trade or service area.
Accurate distance-based discovery requires real coordinates and a working location or geocoding implementation. A manually entered area or sample coordinate should not be presented as verified precise location data.

Database Layer
A proposed database structure may include the following models:

User — account information and user role.
WorkerProfile — trade, skills, experience, service area, and availability.
CustomerProfile — customer-specific information.
Job — job descriptions, requirements, and status.
Booking — worker, customer, date, time slot, and booking status.
Review — customer feedback associated with completed services.
If PostgreSQL and Prisma are adopted, the schema and migrations can live under:

backend/prisma/schema.prisma
backend/prisma/migrations/
Database queries can be organized under /backend/src/database, for example:

workerQueries.ts
customerQueries.ts
jobQueries.ts
bookingQueries.ts
reviewQueries.ts
These models and files are a suggested design, not confirmation that they already exist.

Middleware & Helpers
middleware/auth.ts — validates authentication tokens and protects private endpoints once authentication is implemented.
helpers/passwordHelper.ts — provides secure password-hashing and verification utilities.
helpers/tokenHelper.ts — manages token creation and verification if JWT authentication is used.
helpers/responseHelper.ts — standardizes API response formats.
utils/validation.ts — validates request data.
utils/dateUtils.ts — formats dates and handles booking dates and time slots.
utils/prisma.ts — exports a shared Prisma client if Prisma is configured.
utils/asyncHandler.ts — simplifies asynchronous route error handling.
Security, Logging & Operations
Validate incoming requests on the server.
Protect private endpoints with authentication and role-based authorization.
Keep API keys and database credentials in environment variables.
Avoid collecting or exposing unnecessary personal information.
Record relevant application errors to support debugging.
Use secure storage and access controls for any identity-verification data.
Configure monitoring and automated deployment as the project grows.
Implementation Status
Shramik's current prototype uses React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Vite, Express, and Gemini integration. The routes, services, database models, authentication middleware, and background jobs listed above describe the intended backend architecture and should be marked as implemented only after they have been built and tested.

